Short answer ‚Äî your "correct patch" stores escalation as two scalar fields (`escalationDelayMinutes` + `escalationNotificationId`) while my earlier suggestion used an array of objects (`escalationRules` with `{ notification, delayMinutes }`). That mismatch is why values didn‚Äôt persist.

What the correct patch does differently
- Persists escalation as scalar fields on the Monitor model (`escalationDelayMinutes`, `escalationNotificationId`) and exposes them through validation, types and form defaults.
- My prior fix converted the form to use an array shape and tried to persist `escalationRules` (DB schema with `notification` ObjectId). If the backend expects the scalars, sending the array alone won‚Äôt save the scalars and vice‚Äëversa.

Two safe fixes (pick one)
1. Keep the scalar approach (your patch) ‚Äî update the client submit to send the scalar fields (and optionally also send the DB array for compatibility).
2. Use the array approach everywhere ‚Äî update server schema + repo to accept array shape (more flexible for multiple rules).

Fast, compatible fix
Send both shapes on submit so older/newer code works. Apply this small change to `onSubmit` in `client/src/Pages/CreateMonitor/index.tsx` so the payload includes the scalar fields (what your patch adds) and the DB-style `escalationRules` array (what my earlier changes used). This guarantees persistence regardless of backend shape.
